#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# pivot

#% text
# `pivot(ds, r, c, v)` gives a dataset with a row for each value of column `r` and a column for each value of column `c`, holding the value of column `v` where they meet.

#% text
# `pivot(ds, r, c, v, f)` gives the aggregate function `f` of the values of column `v` where they meet.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Rows and columns come in the order of their first occurrence; the first column is `r`.

#% item
# Without `f` each pair of values may occur once; a pair that does not occur gives `missing()`.

#% item
# `f` is an aggregate function as in `aggregate`: `total`, `mean`, `count`, `min`, `max` or any function.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
trips = dataset([["day", "mode", "km"], ["mon", "bike", 12], ["mon", "bus", 8], ["tue", "bike", 15], ["wed", "bus", 9]])
pivot(trips, "day", "mode", "km")
#% output
#= day |    bike |     bus
#= ----+---------+--------
#= mon |      12 |       8
#= tue |      15 | missing
#= wed | missing |       9
#= dataset: 3 rows, 3 columns

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Several values per cell, added up:

#% cell
more = join(trips, dataset([["day", "mode", "km"], ["mon", "bike", 3]]))
pivot(more, "mode", "day", "km", total)
#% output
#= mode | mon | tue | wed
#= -----+-----+-----+----
#= bike |  15 |  15 |   0
#= bus  |   8 |   0 |   9
#= dataset: 2 rows, 4 columns

#% cell
pivot(more, "mode", "day", "km", count)
#% output
#= mode | mon | tue | wed
#= -----+-----+-----+----
#= bike |   2 |   1 |   0
#= bus  |   1 |   0 |   1
#= dataset: 2 rows, 4 columns

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Without a function, a pair may not repeat:

#% cell
pivot(more, "mode", "day", "km")
#% output
#! failure(duplicate_entry, ["bike", "mon"])

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `aggregate`, `group_by`, `dataset`
